    "created_at": "2016-03-11T00:14:31Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-03-11T00:14:31Z",
    "body": "This would apply if a page could open a popup with some other site in it (say a bank), then use window.open to target the same window to load a javascript: URL. If that were possible then the javascript: code could run with the origin of the bank's page as its origin. There seems to be:\n\nhttps://html.spec.whatwg.org/#navigating-across-documents:javascript-protocol\n\nthough that prevents javascript: from navigating a browser context that has a different origin to itself so it looks like that shuts this exploit down.\n\nThat said, it is really strange that the spec text I originally quoted is written in this way to make it sound like there is a security issue. It would seem better to use the same text as is used for \"If a Document was generated from a data: URL found in another Document or in a script\". If that isn't possible for some reason, at the very least there should be a note explaining why this isn't a security issue.\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
